---
/* /privacy: must stay true of the code. Analytics: src/components/Analytics.astro and src/lib/track.ts. Form: src/pages/contact.astro. */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../styles/pages/legal.css';
import Closing from '../components/Closing.astro';

import { site } from '../data/site';
import { plainText, wordSpans } from '../lib/words';

const heading = `What this site <b>collects.</b>`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy | Nixon Creative Studio"
  description="What this site collects when you visit or use the contact form, what I do with it, and who handles it, including Cloudflare and Google Analytics."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <h1 aria-label={plainText(heading)} set:html={wordSpans(heading)} />
    <p class="lede">
      Short version: a contact form, two tools that count visits (one from Cloudflare that uses no
      cookies, and Google Analytics), and the things any web server logs. No advertising trackers.
      If you have questions, email <a href={site.emailHref}>{site.email}</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="updated">Last updated November 10, 2026</p>
  </header>
  <div class="doc">
    <nav class="toc card" aria-label="On this page">
      <p>On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="#contact-form">Contact form</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#analytics">Analytics</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#server-logs">Server logs</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#cookies">Cookies</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#your-data">Your data</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="#changes">Changes</a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="contact-form">Contact form</h2>
      <p>
        When you submit the form on the Start a project page, the fields you fill in go to this
        site's own server on Cloudflare. It saves them in a small database there, so an inquiry is
        never lost if an email goes astray, and then emails them to my inbox through Cloudflare. If
        that email fails, a backup service,{' '}
        <a href="https://web3forms.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          Web3Forms
        </a>
        , delivers it instead and does not keep it afterwards. I keep inquiries the same way I'd
        keep any project email, and I'll delete yours from both places if you ask.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fields are your name, email, organization type and what you are hoping to build, plus
        anything you add from the optional ones: who will keep it up to date, a budget range, and
        under "A few optional details" your organization's name, phone, current website, timeline
        and how you heard about me. Four hidden fields go with it, filled only when the link that
        brought you to the form carried them: <code>from</code> (the page or case study you came
        from), <code>tier</code> (a price tier you picked), <code>service</code> (a service you
        picked) and <code>origin_org_type</code> (the kind of organization that link named). They
        tell me where an inquiry started, nothing more.
      </p>
      <p>
        The form also includes a hidden "honeypot" field that real visitors never fill in. If a bot
        submits the form and trips the honeypot, the message is quietly discarded.
      </p>
      <h2 id="analytics">Analytics</h2>
      <p>
        The site uses two analytics tools, and I use both for the same thing: to see which pages are
        useful and which aren't.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a
          href="https://www.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Cloudflare Web Analytics
        </a>{' '}
        counts page views and basic performance metrics without cookies, without fingerprinting, and
        without sending data to any third-party advertising network.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a
          href="https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/6004245"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Google Analytics 4
        </a>{' '}
        records page views, scrolls, and outbound clicks, and sends them to Google. It sets
        first-party cookies to tell returning visits apart, and it truncates IP addresses before
        storing anything. I do not use it for advertising, I have not linked it to any ad account,
        and I have not turned on Google Signals or any demographic reporting. To opt out everywhere,
        install Google's{' '}
        <a
          href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          browser add-on
        </a>
        ; most tracker-blocking extensions and browsers also stop it.
      </p>
      <p>
        On top of page views, Google Analytics gets a handful of named events, so I can tell which
        parts of the site lead to a conversation: <code>generate_lead</code> (the contact form went
        through), <code>contact_click</code> (an email or phone link),{' '}
        <code>visit_client_site</code> (a link out to a client's live site from a case study) and{' '}
        <code>pricing_view</code> (a pricing section came into view), <code>tool_use</code> (the
        first time you try one of the site's tools, such as the tier picker or the cost receipt),
        and three for the contact form: <code>inquiry_start</code>, <code>inquiry_step</code> (which
        question you reached) and <code>inquiry_error</code> (a send that failed). Each one carries
        only a fixed label, such as where on the page the click was or which client site it went to.
        When the form goes through, <code>generate_lead</code> also carries the options you picked
        from the lists (organization type, budget range, timing, how you heard about me), never
        anything you typed, your name, your email address or the text of a message.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I can tell which channels bring real inquiries, this site keeps one small note in your
        browser's own storage on your first visit: the page you landed on, the name of the site that
        sent you (just the hostname, like google.com) and any campaign tags in the link. It stays on
        your device and is not sent anywhere on its own. If you send the contact form, that note
        goes with your message, along with how many pages you looked at, so I can see how you found
        me. Clearing your browser's site data removes it.
      </p>
      <h2 id="server-logs">Server logs</h2>
      <p>
        The hosting platform (Cloudflare) records standard request data: IP address, user agent,
        requested URL, response code, timestamp. These logs are retained for a short window and used
        for operational purposes like blocking abuse and diagnosing errors. They are not used to
        build profiles of visitors.
      </p>
      <h2 id="cookies">Cookies</h2>
      <p>
        Google Analytics sets a small number of first-party cookies (the <code>_ga</code> family) so
        it can tell a returning visit from a new one. They carry a random identifier, not your name
        or email, and the Analytics section above links the opt-out. The fonts are served from this
        site, so no font service sees your visit. Cloudflare, which hosts the site, may also set a
        short-lived security cookie called <code>cf_clearance</code> when it checks that a visit
        comes from a real browser rather than a bot; it is used only for that check. Nothing else on
        the site sets cookies. If a future change adds more third-party content, this page will say
        so first.
      </p>
      <h2 id="your-data">Your data</h2>
      <p>
        If you've sent a project inquiry and want me to delete it from my inbox, email{' '}
        <a href={site.emailHref}>{site.email}</a> with the email address you used to send it. I'll
        confirm when it's deleted.
      </p>
      <h2 id="changes">Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If what the site collects changes, I'll update this page and bump the "Last updated" date at
        the top. There's no separate notification list for privacy updates.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <Closing />
</BaseLayout>
